22\/house\/ne\/nebraska_2nd_district_-7429.html\">NE-2<\/a><strong><br \/>2020: <\/strong><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/president\/ne\/nebraska_trump_vs_biden-7280.html\">President<\/a> | <a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/president\/necd2\/nebraska_cd2_trump_vs_biden-7279.html\">President CD 2<\/a> | <a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/senate\/ne\/nebraska_senate_sasse_vs_democrat-7076.html\">Senate<\/a> | <a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2020\/house\/ne\/nebraska_2nd_district_bacon_vs_eastman-7128.html\">NE-2<\/a><strong><br \/>2018:<\/strong> <a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/senate\/ne\/nebraska_senate_fischer_vs_raybould_-6315.html\">Senate<\/a> | <a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2018\/house\/ne\/nebraska_2nd_district_bacon_vs_eastman-6381.html\">NE-2<\/a><strong><br \/>2016:<span> <\/span><\/strong><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/president\/ne\/nebraska_trump_vs_clinton_vs_johnson_vs_stein-6147.html\">President<\/a> | <a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/president\/necd2\/nebraska_cd2_trump_vs_clinton-5906.html\">CD2<\/a><strong><br \/>2014: <\/strong><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/governor\/ne\/nebraska_governor_ricketts_vs_hassebrook-5045.html\">Governor<\/a> | <a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/senate\/ne\/nebraska_senate_sasse_vs_domina-5004.html\">Senate<\/a><span> <\/span>| <a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/house\/ne\/nebraska_2nd_district_terry_vs_ashford-5050.html\">NE-2<\/a><strong><br \/><\/strong><\/p>"},"dataAnalysis":{"title":"Race Analysis","content":"<p>Nebraska was one of a handful of states that voted against Franklin Roosevelt twice, and from 1940-2024 it voted for a Democratic presidential candidate only once (it gave LBJ 52 percent of the vote in 1964). But Democrats have had more luck at the gubernatorial level: Republicans won only three of 12 such elections from 1958 through 1994.<\/p>\n<p>This translated into surprising Democratic strength in Senate races. From 1978 through 1994, Nebraska sent only Democrats to Washington. Edward Zorinsky was a Republican mayor of Omaha who switched parties because he thought he couldn\u2019t win the Republican nomination. As a senator, he was a moderate-to-conservative Democrat, voting with Republicans on some significant issues. (He was courted by the GOP to rejoin the party in 1982, and hinted he might indeed switch back in 1986, but ultimately never did.) Jim Exon, Bob Kerrey, and Ben Nelson were former governors who cut heterodox profiles in Washington. All told, between 1976 and 2006, Democrats won nine of 11 Senate races in the Cornhusker State.<\/p>\n<p>But just as the 1994 election seems to have marked the end of Democratic gubernatorial dominance, the 2012 election seemed to mark the end of the Democratic Senate dynasty. Democrats nominated former senator and governor Kerrey to replace the retiring Nelson. Republicans nominated Deb Fischer, a member of Nebraska's unique unicameral legislature. Fischer won by over 16 points, giving Nebraska two Republican senators for the first time in almost 40 years. Democrats haven\u2019t come particularly close since.<\/p>\n<p>In 2024, Democrats didn\u2019t even try: The party declined to nominate anyone against Fischer. Independent Dan Osborn, a steamfitter who led a major strike in Omaha, held her to a seven-point win. That was the best showing against a Nebraska Republican in almost two decades (when former Sen. Ben Nelson defeated Pete Ricketts).<\/p>\n<p>Osborn is back this time, running against Sen. Pete Ricketts. Ricketts, a former governor, is generally seen as a more formidable candidate than Fischer. He was appointed to the seat vacated by former Sen. Ben Sasse, then won a 2024 special election by 25 points. Osborn\u2019s campaign is largely built around the idea that Ricketts effectively bought the seat when he campaigned and fundraised for the governor who later appointed Ricketts. Osborn claims that both parties are bought and paid for, and that by refusing party labeling he will be neither. Democrats have again declined to field a candidate: their primary winner withdrew in July, and the state party is united behind Osborn. Republicans, of course, dispute whether Osborn is truly an independent, and have quite a bit of ammunition to suggest that Osborn will for all intents and purposes be a Democrat in DC.<\/p>\n<p>Democrats are trying to execute the same play across the Plains and the Mountain West. It\u2019s essentially a \u201creverse Zorinsky gambit\u201d: Democrats are lending their voters to candidates who want nothing to do with the label.\u00a0Whether it can work is a different question. We\u2019ve seen seemingly promising independent candidacies fall apart before in Alaska, Kansas and South Dakota. Voters eventually realize that the candidate will have to get committee assignments from <em>some <\/em>party.<\/p>\n<p>Osborn is not going to be underfunded this time, but he topped out at 47 percent in 2024 against a considerably less wealthy opponent whom he caught napping, and getting those last four points in a state Donald Trump carried by 20 is the hard part. Ricketts still has to be favored. If he isn\u2019t winning comfortably by late October, Republicans have a much bigger problem than Nebraska.<\/p>"},"showLineChart":0,"showBarChart":0,"showLineChartFirst":0,"sideCharts":{"charts_on_top":0,"left_chart_race_id":"7076","right_chart_race_id":"5004"},"show_final_results":0,"chart_data":{"chart_id":"7592","chart_axis_x":null,"chart_grid_x":null,"chart_axis_y":null,"chart_grid_y":null,"chart_min":null,"chart_max":null,"chart_start":null,"chart_end":null,"poll_id":"9035","chart_highlight":null,"chart_days_extend":"7","chart_height":"360","chart_height_mobile":"360","chart_html5":